{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 24, :comments ["<b>And he was not.</b> Because his days were short compared to the others, it appears as if he was not." "<b>And he was not, for Elohim took him.</b> Onkelos translated: ואיתוהי ארי לא אמית יתיה — 'and he is, for He did not kill him.' That is, he still stands and exists, for HaShem did not kill him, and he is destined to come with Elijah at the time of redemption. And every instance of 'אין' and 'ואיננו' in the Talmud means something else, not this 'ואיננו.' And in the Jerusalem Targum they translate: והא לית הוא ארי איתנגיד במימר מן קדם ה' — 'behold he is not, for he was removed by a decree from before HaShem.' According to the Jerusalem Targum, one must say <b>took him</b> is an expression of death, as in 'קח נא את נפשי,' 'אך אלקים יפדה נפשי,' 'מיד שאול כי יקחני סלה' — yet this was an honorable way, for he was a righteous man."], :book :genesis, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T15:03:06.765351077Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}